DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'MathObjects.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');
TEXT(beginproblem());
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;
$prompt = <<'PROMPT_C708_9296';
<p>c708_9296</p> <table border="0" style="border: 0px solid white; border-collapse: collapse; "><colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <tr><td rowspan="3"></td><td align="center">Gene 1</td></tr><tr><td align="center"><span style="color: red;"><b>&#10005;</b></span>&darr;<span style="color: red;"><b>&#10005;</b></span></td></tr><tr><td align="center">Gene 2</td></tr><tr> <td align="center" style="background-color: ivory">white</td> <td align="center">&xrarr;</td> <td align="center" style="background-color: cornflowerblue">blue</td></tr></table><br/>When gene 2 is dominant and gene 1 is homozygous recessive, gene 2 expresses an active enzyme that converts the white precursor pigment into the blue pigment. Whenever gene 1 is dominant, then gene 1 expresses a protein that completely suppresses the activity of gene 2. When both genes are dominant, the inhibited gene 2 is unable to produce the blue pigment, so the white precursor pigment remains. If neither of the genes are dominant, then there is no active enzyme and again only the white precursor pigment remains. <br/> <br/><p>The diagram and description above explain the interaction of two genes. <b>Determine the dihybird cross phenotypic ratio.</b></p>
PROMPT_C708_9296
loadMacros('parserRadioButtons.pl');
$choice_1 = <<'LITERAL_C708_9296_CHOICE_1';
